\section*{Alignment-Based Information Modelling (AIM)}

Railway alignments are traditionally described as geometric curves in
space. However, this view is insufficient for modern engineering tasks,
which require the integration of design, measurement, construction,
and optimization.

This work introduces \emph{Alignment-Based Information Modelling (AIM)}
as a unified framework that treats alignments not as static curves,
but as the result of a structured transformation process.

---

\section*{Core Idea}

The central concept of AIM is to represent alignments through curvature
as a function of arc-length:
\[
\kappa(s).
\]

This intrinsic representation:
\begin{itemize}
\item directly connects geometry and dynamics,
\item provides a low-dimensional and structured model,
\item enables efficient optimization.
\end{itemize}

---

\section*{From Geometry to System}

Instead of a single model, AIM defines a pipeline of operators:
\[
\mathcal{P}
=
\mathcal{W}^{-1}
\circ
\mathcal{R}
\circ
\mathcal{M}
\circ
\mathcal{W}
\circ
\mathcal{C}.
\]

This pipeline links:
\begin{itemize}
\item coordinate systems (CRS),
\item world geometry,
\item intrinsic alignment coordinates,
\item parametric and hybrid modelling,
\item physical realization,
\item and optimization.
\end{itemize}

Thus, alignment modelling becomes a \emph{process} rather than a static
representation.

---

\section*{Hybrid Modelling}

AIM combines parametric structure with local flexibility:
\[
\kappa(s)
=
\kappa_{\mathrm{param}}(s;\mathbf{T})
+
\delta\kappa(s).
\]

This hybrid representation:
\begin{itemize}
\item preserves engineering intent,
\item accommodates imperfect measurement data,
\item avoids over-parameterization.
\end{itemize}

---

\section*{Realization Matters}

A key insight is that the designed alignment is not identical to the
constructed track.

A realization operator
\[
\mathcal{R}
\]
transforms the theoretical model into physically realizable geometry,
introducing smoothing and constraints.

\textbf{Therefore, design must be performed in realized space.}

---

\section*{Optimization}

The alignment problem is formulated as a structured nonlinear
least-squares problem and solved using Sequential Quadratic Programming
(SQP).

Due to:
\begin{itemize}
\item locality in arc-length,
\item sparse Jacobians,
\item intrinsic block structure,
\end{itemize}
the optimization is computationally efficient and scalable.

---

\section*{World-to-Track Transformation}

A central component of AIM is the transformation between global
coordinates and intrinsic track coordinates:
\[
x \leftrightarrow (s,d).
\]

This nonlinear mapping:
\begin{itemize}
\item connects measurement data to the alignment model,
\item introduces scale-dependent complexity,
\item requires hybrid and LOD-based strategies.
\end{itemize}

---

\section*{Key Principles}

The framework is governed by the following principles:

\begin{itemize}
\item curvature-centric modelling,
\item separation of model and realization,
\item hybrid parametric--discrete representation,
\item operator-based architecture,
\item optimization in realized space,
\item exploitation of sparsity and structure,
\item integration of measurement via world-to-track mapping,
\item acceptance of non-uniqueness.
\end{itemize}

---

\section*{Outcome}

AIM provides a coherent foundation for:
\begin{itemize}
\item alignment reconstruction from imperfect data,
\item physically meaningful optimization,
\item integration of design, measurement, and construction,
\item scalable computational implementation.
\end{itemize}

---

\section*{Conclusion}

Alignment modelling is not merely a geometric task.

It is the integration of:
\begin{itemize}
\item geometry,
\item physics,
\item data,
\item and computation.
\end{itemize}

AIM formalizes this integration as a structured operator pipeline,
establishing a new perspective on railway alignment engineering.
